    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Record quarter and $20B deal, but Nvidia turmoil drags COHR below every EMA",
               "summary":  "Coherent\u0027s business is still growing fast, but the stock is falling. Fiscal Q1 2027 results (reported 4 Nov 2026) showed revenue of $2.37B, up 50% YoY. The company also signed a $20B five-year PhotonLink deal and guided Q2 revenue to $2.55-2.75B. Even so, the shares fell about 13% on 5 Nov and another 12.9% to $187.22 on 20 Nov. That second drop came when reports said the SEC was reviewing disclosures at Nvidia, Coherent\u0027s anchor customer and a $2B investor, shortly after Jensen Huang resigned as Nvidia\u0027s CEO. The price is below all three EMAs and 57% under its $440 52-week high.",
               "pros":  [
                            "Q1 FY27 revenue was $2.37B, up 50%, with datacenter \u0026 communications up 79% to about $1.95B.",
                            "A new $20B five-year PhotonLink deal with an unnamed AI customer starts producing revenue in early 2028.",
                            "Q2 guidance is $2.55-2.75B, and management targets a $3B quarter by the end of FY27 and a $4B quarter in FY28.",
                            "In-house indium phosphide laser capacity doubled year on year and should more than double again by end-2027.",
                            "The CEO and the Chairman together bought about $1.2M of stock in the week before 16 Nov."
                        ],
               "cons":  [
                            "Capex was $776M in one quarter, trailing free cash flow was -$1.84B, and debt is $4.35B.",
                            "The stock trades as an Nvidia proxy, and Nvidia faces a CEO exit and a reported (unconfirmed) SEC review.",
                            "The 40.6% non-GAAP gross margin is still well short of the \u003e45% goal set for end-2028.",
                            "Marvell\u0027s $115B all-stock deal for Lumentum creates a larger vertically integrated rival.",
                            "COHR has had three double-digit daily moves in three weeks and trades below all its EMAs."
                        ],
               "opportunityCheck":  "The scores are mostly reasonable, but AI revenue exposure looks too low. Datacenter \u0026 communications made up about 82% of Q1 FY27 revenue and grew 79%, so AI-driven optics now dominate the mix. I would move exposure up the most. Optionality could also rise a little, because the $20B PhotonLink deal shows a newer platform turning into contracted revenue. The bottleneck score fits: customers prepay to secure indium phosphide laser capacity. Position is worth watching once the Marvell-Lumentum merger closes.",
               "technical":  "Trend: the price of $187.22 is far below the EMA50 ($245.07), EMA100 ($268.02) and EMA200 ($265.67). EMAs are moving averages that give more weight to recent days. The EMA50 has crossed below both longer EMAs, which is a typical downtrend pattern. Momentum: MACD 12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge, is -13.12 against a signal line of -14.73. That gives a histogram of +1.61, so selling pressure had been easing before 20 Nov, but the readings are still deeply negative and do not show a reversal. RSI 14, a 0-100 momentum gauge where under 30 means oversold, is 38.92: weak but not oversold. Volatility: the Bollinger Bands (the 20-day average plus or minus 2 standard deviations) run from $177.23 to $249.11, and the price is only about $10 above the lower band. It sits in the bottom fifth of the $130.42-$440 52-week range. Volume: 14.18M shares traded, about 1.36x the 20-day average, so volume confirmed the 20 Nov drop from a $207 open to near the $180.43 low. Support is at $180.43, $177.23, and then the 5 Nov close near $169.76. Resistance is at $207-213, then the EMA50 near $245.",
               "fundamental":  "Coherent is one of the few optics makers that builds its own indium phosphide lasers, chips and finished 800G/1.6T transceivers. For fiscal Q1 2027 (quarter to 30 Sep), revenue was $2.37B, up 50% YoY. Non-GAAP gross margin was 40.6% (38.7% a year earlier), and non-GAAP EPS was $2.09 (vs $1.16). For Q2 it guided revenue to $2.55-2.75B and EPS to $2.28-2.48. It expects more than 60% pro-forma growth in FY27 and says calendar 2027 is nearly fully booked. The 1.6T ramp is under way with multiple customers. The $20B PhotonLink agreement adds a second anchor customer alongside Nvidia. The cost is high: $776M of capex in one quarter, negative operating cash flow and $4.35B of debt. Turning that spending into cash is the key test at the February 2027 report.",
               "news":  [
                            "4 Nov 2026: Q1 FY27 revenue was $2.37B (+50%), non-GAAP EPS $2.09 and Q2 guidance $2.55-2.75B. The 1.6T transceivers are ramping with multiple customers, and in-house InP capacity doubled year on year.",
                            "4 Nov 2026: Coherent signed a roughly $20B five-year PhotonLink agreement with an unnamed AI customer, with revenue starting in early calendar 2028. Management called it a second anchor customer alongside Nvidia.",
                            "5-6 Nov 2026: The shares fell about 13% on 5 Nov despite the beat, in a broad sell-off of AI optics stocks, then bounced about 13.6% on 6 Nov. J.P. Morgan reiterated its $385 target.",
                            "Early Nov 2026: Marvell agreed to buy Lumentum in an all-stock deal valued at about $115B, creating a larger rival that combines lasers and DSP chips. The deal is expected to close in the first half of 2028.",
                            "10-12 Nov 2026: A draft FCC rule would ban new models of Chinese-made optical transceivers, and Microsoft lobbied the White House against it. COHR held at $234.44 on 12 Nov after a 9% gain on 11 Nov.",
                            "Week before 16 Nov 2026: CEO Jim Anderson and Chairman Enrico DiGirolamo bought about $1.2M of shares. The stock still fell 6.83% on 16 Nov as Nvidia\u0027s CEO change weighed on AI suppliers.",
                            "20 Nov 2026: COHR fell 12.9% to $187.22 with no news specific to Coherent. The drop came after a WSJ report, not confirmed by the SEC or Nvidia, that the SEC is reviewing Nvidia\u0027s disclosures; Nvidia invested $2B in Coherent in 2026."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The latest falls came from the stock\u0027s link to Nvidia and from cash burn, not from weaker demand. Even so, both can hurt for months. Trailing free cash flow is -$1.84B and debt is $4.35B, so if AI capex slows, Coherent could be left with expensive capacity. If the reported SEC review or the CEO change at Nvidia leads to delayed orders, COHR would likely react sharply. Price is below all EMAs, volume confirmed the sell-off, and support at $177-180 is close. A break below it would leave the $169.76 low from 5 Nov as the next reference point.",
               "opportunity":  "The company is executing well while the stock sells off. Revenue is growing 50%, calendar 2027 is nearly booked, customers are booking into 2028, and a $20B contract has been added. Analysts\u0027 median target was $420 before 20 Nov, and the 20 Nov drop had no company-specific trigger. In-house InP capacity is doubling again, which supports both margin targets and supply security. A draft US curb on Chinese transceivers could also tilt share toward US makers. A long-term holder may see the gap between fundamentals and price as worth tracking, but needs to watch cash conversion.",
               "risks":  [
